Why Idempotency is Needed in Kafka

Kafka guarantees at-least-once delivery by default.

That means a message can be delivered more than once.

Example:

Producer → Kafka → Consumer

Scenario:

Consumer processes a payment event

Consumer crashes before committing offset

Kafka re-delivers the same message

Now the consumer may process the same payment twice ❌

Example Problem (Without Idempotency)

Message in Kafka topic:

{
  "transaction_id": "TX1001",
  "user_id": 25,
  "amount": 1000
}

Consumer processes it:

Deduct 1000 from user wallet

Consumer crashes before committing offset.

Kafka sends message again.

Result:

Deduct 1000 again

Total deducted = 2000 ❌

Solution: Idempotent Consumer

The consumer checks whether the transaction was already processed.

Step 1 — Store transaction ID

Example database table:

transaction_id	status
TX1001	PROCESSED
Step 2 — Consumer logic

Pseudo logic:

Receive message
      ↓
Check transaction_id in database
      ↓
If exists → ignore message
If not exists → process transaction
      ↓
Store transaction_id
      ↓
Commit offset
Example Consumer Flow

Kafka message:

{
  "transaction_id": "TX1001",
  "amount": 1000
}

Consumer steps:

Check DB → TX1001 exists?

Case 1 — First time:

Not found
→ Process payment
→ Save TX1001

Case 2 — Duplicate message:

Found in DB
→ Ignore message

Result:

Payment executed only once
Example Code Logic (Pseudo)
if (!transactionRepository.exists(txId)) {

    processPayment();

    transactionRepository.save(txId);

}

This makes the consumer idempotent.

Another Approach (Unique Constraint)

Create unique constraint in database:

ALTER TABLE transactions
ADD CONSTRAINT unique_tx_id UNIQUE(transaction_id);

If duplicate message arrives:

INSERT fails

Consumer ignores duplicate.

Interview Tip (Very Important)

If interviewer asks:

How do you handle duplicate messages in Kafka?

Best answer:

Kafka provides at-least-once delivery, so duplicate messages can occur. To handle this, consumers should be idempotent. This is usually done by storing a unique transaction ID or event ID in a database and checking it before processing the message.

Fintech Example (Easypaisa Type System)

Money transfer event:

User sends money
      ↓
Transaction Service
      ↓
Kafka Topic: transactions
      ↓
Wallet Service (Consumer)

Wallet service logic:

Check transaction_id
If processed → ignore
Else → update wallet balance

This ensures no double deduction.